---
import TopicLayout from '../../layouts/TopicLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<TopicLayout
  title="Champagne"
  subtitle="Sparkling Wine · Northeastern France"
  parentLabel="Regions"
  parentPath={`${base}regions/`}
  description="A comprehensive guide to Champagne — the region, the grapes, the method, and the great producers."
  stats={[
    { value: '~34,000 ha', label: 'Vineyard area' },
    { value: '319', label: 'Villages (crus)' },
    { value: '150 km', label: 'From Paris' },
    { value: '~10.8 °C', label: 'Avg. growing temp.' },
    { value: 'Min. 15 months', label: 'Aging (NV)' },
    { value: 'Min. 36 months', label: 'Aging (Vintage)' },
  ]}
  toc={[
    { id: 'overview',    label: 'Overview' },
    { id: 'region',      label: 'The Region' },
    { id: 'terroir',     label: 'Terroir & Climate' },
    { id: 'grapes',      label: 'The Grapes' },
    { id: 'production',  label: 'Production Method' },
    { id: 'styles',      label: 'Styles' },
    { id: 'label',       label: 'Reading the Label' },
    { id: 'producers',   label: 'Notable Producers' },
    { id: 'price',       label: 'Price Guide' },
    { id: 'pairing',     label: 'Food Pairing' },
  ]}
>

  <!-- OVERVIEW -->
  <section id="overview" class="topic-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Overview</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">
      Champagne is the world's most celebrated sparkling wine, produced exclusively in the northernmost wine region of France. Its quality rests on three pillars: a unique chalk subsoil, a cool marginal climate that preserves natural acidity, and centuries of blending expertise that has no parallel anywhere else in the wine world.
    </p>
    <div class="section-body">
      <p>Unlike most fine wines, which celebrate a single vineyard and a single vintage, the majority of Champagne is non-vintage — a deliberate act of blending across grape varieties, villages, and years. The objective is consistency and house style, not the expression of one place or one harvest. This philosophy is what makes the chef de cave (cellar master) one of the most important figures in fine wine.</p>
      <p>The Champagne appellation is strictly controlled. Only wines produced within the delimited zone, from permitted grape varieties, using the traditional method, may be called Champagne. Everything else — however good — is sparkling wine.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE REGION -->
  <section id="region" class="topic-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">The Region</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">Located 150 km northeast of Paris, Champagne sits at the very northern edge of viable viticulture in France. The region comprises five distinct sub-zones, each with different soils, exposures, and grape specialisations.</p>

    <!-- SVG Map -->
